WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.545
Querying is where a hunt hypothesis becomes an evidence search.

00:00:03.545 --> 00:00:08.367
The exact syntax depends on the SIEM, data lake, EDR console, or log platform,

00:00:08.367 --> 00:00:10.089
but the thinking is provider-neutral.

00:00:10.089 --> 00:00:14.223
Start with the behavior, identify the required fields, select the data source,

00:00:14.223 --> 00:00:15.600
define the time window,

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.767
and write a query or filter that returns enough context to reason about the event.

00:00:20.767 --> 00:00:24.556
A good query is not just a string that produces results.

00:00:24.556 --> 00:00:27.656
It is a documented test of a hunt idea.

00:00:27.656 --> 00:00:31.445
Pivots help the analyst move from one clue to related evidence.

00:00:31.445 --> 00:00:36.957
A host-to-user pivot asks which identities were active on a system around a time of interest.

00:00:36.957 --> 00:00:42.123
A user-to-source-IP pivot asks where an account authenticated from and whether that location is normal.

00:00:42.123 --> 00:00:45.223
A process-to-parent-process pivot asks what launched a suspicious command.

00:00:45.223 --> 00:00:48.324
A domain-to-DNS-clients pivot asks which systems resolved a destination.

00:00:48.324 --> 00:00:51.079
A hash-to-endpoints pivot asks where a file appeared.

00:00:51.079 --> 00:00:54.868
An alert-to-timeline pivot asks what happened before and after a signal.

00:00:54.868 --> 00:00:58.313
Each pivot should have a purpose tied to the hypothesis.

00:00:58.313 --> 00:01:01.413
Noise reduction is necessary, but it can be dangerous.

00:01:01.413 --> 00:01:04.857
Large environments produce routine administrative tools, software updates, cloud automation,

00:01:04.857 --> 00:01:09.335
service accounts, vulnerability scanners, and user behavior that may look odd in isolation.

00:01:09.335 --> 00:01:13.469
Filters help remove known benign patterns, but over-filtering can hide the signal.

00:01:13.469 --> 00:01:17.602
If you exclude all administrative accounts, you may miss compromised administrator activity.

00:01:17.602 --> 00:01:22.080
If you exclude all internal tools, you may miss misuse of those tools.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.902
A better approach is to filter deliberately and document why each exclusion is reasonable.

00:01:26.902 --> 00:01:28.969
Baselines are useful for reducing noise.

00:01:28.969 --> 00:01:31.725
Instead of asking whether a process ever runs,

00:01:31.725 --> 00:01:36.547
ask whether it runs on this host type, under this parent, by this user,

00:01:36.547 --> 00:01:37.925
during this time window.

00:01:37.925 --> 00:01:41.025
Instead of asking whether a domain is rare globally,

00:01:41.025 --> 00:01:45.159
ask whether it is rare for this business unit or endpoint group.

00:01:45.159 --> 00:01:49.292
Baselines are not perfect proof, but they help separate ordinary from unusual.

00:01:49.292 --> 00:01:50.670
Save queries and assumptions.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:53.425
A hunt should be repeatable by another analyst.

00:01:53.425 --> 00:01:57.903
Save the query text, data source, time window, filters, field names, known exclusions,

00:01:57.903 --> 00:01:58.937
and any limitations.

00:01:58.937 --> 00:02:02.037
If a field name changes or a parser breaks,

00:02:02.037 --> 00:02:05.481
future reviewers need to know what the query depended on.

00:02:05.481 --> 00:02:09.270
The beginner mistake is to treat querying as the hunt itself.

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:10.648
Querying is a tool.

00:02:10.648 --> 00:02:14.782
The hunt is the structured process of asking a question, collecting evidence,

00:02:14.782 --> 00:02:18.571
pivoting carefully, reducing noise responsibly, and documenting what the evidence supports.

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:22.015
Provider-neutral thinking helps when teams use more than one tool.

00:02:22.015 --> 00:02:26.149
One SIEM may call a field process_name, another may call it image,

00:02:26.149 --> 00:02:30.626
and an EDR console may show the same concept in a different panel.

00:02:30.626 --> 00:02:33.727
The hunter should understand the concept behind the field.

00:02:33.727 --> 00:02:37.860
That understanding makes the hunt portable and reduces dependence on memorized syntax.
